n the fructose  6-phosphate so this transformation can  be accomplished by an intermediate which  you have seen already in glycolysis the  intermediate that allows to go from  glucose 6-phosphate to fructose  6-phosphate  which is an sis in dial so it's  essentially inhalation with the carbon  with a hydrogen on carbon 2 so if we  wanted to form the enol here which as  you know from the carbonyl video so a  base could deprotonate and allow the  electrons to move to form another  hydroxyl on the one position because  this is a C's sis in dial to the two  hydroxyl are going to end up on the same  side of the double bond  once again let's number our carbons 4 3  2 1 and this is our sis in dye all right  so we have 2 hydroxyls they're both  attached to a double bond and sis means  they're both on the same side of the  molecule and this allows the enzyme to  basically switch back and forth between  which one of these hydroxyl becomes the  carbonyl if we go backwards we have the  carbon yellow that one position however  if we if we go forward we can move the  carbonyl on the 2 position so that's  just the reverse of the inhalation  reaction in which we will deeper Nate  this hydroxyl and repr Nate at carbon-12  take us forward to generate 2 keto group  which is fructose 6-phosphate in the  linear form now from here in the very  last step we just need to close the ring  so that's just a hemiketal formation we  have a base deprotonated hydroxyl  position 5 and this will attack the  ketone to form the hemiketal group that  we see in fructose 6-phosphate so now  this is basically the the curved arrow  mechanism from going from mannose  6-phosphate to fructose 6-phosphate now  some key points to notice here is that  this inhalation reaction where the base  removes this alpha hydrogen would not  have been possible in the cyclic  structures if you look here that this  hydrogen the pKa is about 30 now once we  form the the linear structure here the  pKa of this hydrogen now it's 